## Environment

- flashinfer main `63b24de5` (verified 2026-09-11). The vendored file has not been touched by any commit since #1113 imported it, so the line references below hold for current main.
- Verified on NVIDIA A100-PCIE-40GB (sm_80), driver 575.57.08, nvcc CUDA 12.8.
- The reproduction compiles the vendored CUDA headers directly from a checkout and never imports the flashinfer package, so `python -m flashinfer.collect_env` does not apply here.

## Bug description

The repository carries two copies of TRT-LLM's `cudaTypeUtils.cuh`. The vendored tree under `csrc/nv_internal/tensorrt_llm/` came in with #1113, and #1212 later created the header-only twin under `include/flashinfer/trtllm/`. PR #4167 fixed the packed fp8 conversion in the twin on 2026-07-27, after #4160 reported intermittent `test_layernorm_quant` failures. The fix never crossed to the vendored copy, which still carries the pre-fix conversion at `csrc/nv_internal/tensorrt_llm/common/cudaTypeUtils.cuh:595`.

```cpp
template <>
__device__ inline __nv_fp8x2_e4m3 cuda_cast<__nv_fp8x2_e4m3, float2>(float2 val) {
  return __nv_fp8x2_e4m3(bf1622float2(float22bf162(val)));
}
```

This rounds the f32 value to bf16 first and converts to e4m3 afterwards, so every value passes through two roundings. Quantization kernels apply the scale before the cast, and the mantissa bits dropped by the bf16 step are then amplified by 1/scale. That can push the value across an e4m3 rounding boundary, and the result lands one representable step away from the directly rounded value. It is the failure #4160 caught in the twin, where quant_scale=0.01 pushed the mismatch fraction of some seeds past the 1% bound of the nightly check. The fixed twin converts directly.

```cpp
// include/flashinfer/trtllm/common/cudaTypeUtils.cuh:594-599
template <>
__device__ inline __nv_fp8x2_e4m3 cuda_cast<__nv_fp8x2_e4m3, float2>(float2 val) {
  // Direct conversion: an intermediate bf16 step drops mantissa bits that a
  // small quantization scale then amplifies past e4m3 bucket boundaries.
  __nv_fp8x2_e4m3 out;
  out.__x = __nv_cvt_float2_to_fp8x2(val, __NV_SATFINITE, __NV_E4M3);
  return out;
}
```

Diffing the two files on current main shows only the include prefixes, this conversion, and two e5m2 specializations that exist only in the twin.

Inside flashinfer, the fp8 e4m3 instantiation of this cast is currently dormant. The chain is `perTokenQuantization` (`csrc/nv_internal/tensorrt_llm/kernels/quantization.cuh:132`) calling `quantizeAndStore`, whose `cuda_cast<QuantT>(val2)` at `csrc/nv_internal/tensorrt_llm/kernels/quantization_utils.cuh:103` instantiates this specialization for e4m3, with host entry `invokePerTokenQuantization` (`csrc/nv_internal/cpp/kernels/quantization.cu:242`). `invokePerTokenQuantization` has no callers in the repo, and the trtllm-gen MoE launcher accepts only MxFp8 and DeepSeekFp8 for fp8 (`csrc/trtllm_fused_moe_kernel_launcher.cu:1015`), so no shipped path reaches this cast today. The vendored tree itself is alive, though. The launcher includes its `quantization.h` (line 38), `csrc/nv_internal/tensorrt_llm/kernels/communicationKernels/moeAlltoAllKernels.cu` uses `quantization_utils.cuh`, and #5031 (open) fixes an NVFP4 accuracy regression inside this same vendored `quantization.cuh`. A change that wires the fp8 per-token path back, a new kernel added to this tree, or a downstream build that compiles `csrc/nv_internal` directly would inherit the double rounding.

### Measured impact

Verified on A100-PCIE-40GB by compiling the two casts from the repository sources and comparing them head to head, with the direct conversion `__nv_cvt_float2_to_fp8x2` as the reference.

1. **Where the two casts disagree.** The differences sit exactly at the e4m3 rounding boundaries. In a scan of the ±8 f32-ulp neighborhood of all 252 midpoints between adjacent representable values, 2016 of 4284 points convert differently. Every differing output is exactly one representable step away from the direct conversion, and 0 of 504 bucket-interior control points differ. Over 4.2M values drawn log-uniformly across the e4m3 domain, 2.34% convert differently. The vendored output matches `e4m3.rn(bf16.rn(v))` byte for byte on 100% of the inputs, and the fixed twin matches `e4m3.rn(v)` on 100%, so the disagreement is fully attributed to the intermediate bf16 rounding.
2. **The #4160 failure mode.** The value distribution of the fixed test reference pipeline (`layer_norm`, round to bf16, divide by quant_scale) was fed through the vendored cast with quant_scale=0.01, hidden sizes 500 and 1024, 30 seeds each. Under the nightly criterion, which allows at most 1% of elements outside `isclose(rtol=0.1, atol=0.1)`, 3/30 seeds fail for hidden=500 (worst 1.400%) and 3/30 for hidden=1024 (worst 1.172%). For scale 1.0 and 10.0 all seeds pass with 0.00% mismatch. The #4167 author measured 4/30 (worst 1.20%) and 3/30 (worst 1.27%) before the fix on RTX A6000, the same shape and magnitude.
3. **The dormant epilogue.** The actual vendored `quantizeAndStore<float2, __nv_fp8x2_e4m3>`, the quantization end of `perTokenQuantization` for float activations with dynamic per-token scales between 90 and 166, was driven on real activations. 3.16% of its outputs (64×4096) and 2.95% (64×512) differ from the direct conversion, and substituting the fixed cast makes the same kernel agree with it on 100% of outputs.

The failure stays silent at the element level. The outputs are valid e4m3 values one representable step apart, each inside the fp8 tolerance, which is why #4160 only surfaced through the aggregate mismatch-fraction check.

## Minimal reproduction

The cast is a CUDA header specialization that no Python entry point reaches, so the reproduction compiles the vendored header directly from a checkout and compares it against the direct conversion. It needs no inputs and no RNG, because the test points are derived from the e4m3 format itself. `ENABLE_FP8` and `ENABLE_BF16` gate the fp8 and bf16 sections of the vendored headers and are set by the normal build.

```bash
git clone https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer && cd flashinfer
# save repro.cu from the details block below
nvcc -std=c++17 -arch=sm_80 -DENABLE_FP8 -DENABLE_BF16 repro.cu -o repro \
    -I csrc/nv_internal -I csrc/nv_internal/include
./repro
```

Expected output (A100, CUDA 12.8):

```text
finite e4m3 values: 253, adjacent pairs: 252

  v=-431.999969  vendored=0xfe (-448)  direct=0xfd (-416)
  v=-431.999939  vendored=0xfe (-448)  direct=0xfd (-416)
  v=-431.999908  vendored=0xfe (-448)  direct=0xfd (-416)
  v=-431.999878  vendored=0xfe (-448)  direct=0xfd (-416)
  v=-431.999847  vendored=0xfe (-448)  direct=0xfd (-416)
  v=-431.999817  vendored=0xfe (-448)  direct=0xfd (-416)
boundary points: 4284, vendored != direct: 2016 (47.06%), max step distance: 1

control points: 504, vendored != direct: 0 (0.00%), max step distance: 0
```

For every test point the probe runs both the vendored `cuda_cast<__nv_fp8x2_e4m3, float2>` and the direct `__nv_cvt_float2_to_fp8x2`. The boundary rows cover the midpoints between all adjacent representable e4m3 values plus up to 8 f32-ulps to either side, and the control rows sit deep inside the buckets. The vendored cast disagrees with the direct conversion on 47% of the boundary points, never in the bucket interiors, and never by more than one representable step. Applying the #4167 change to the vendored file makes the two columns identical, since the fixed cast is exactly the direct conversion.

<details><summary>repro.cu</summary>

```cpp
// Minimal probe for the vendored TRT-LLM fp8 packed cast in flashinfer.
//
// The vendored copy of cudaTypeUtils.cuh under csrc/nv_internal still converts
// float2 -> __nv_fp8x2_e4m3 through bf16 (double rounding); the
// include/flashinfer/trtllm twin of the same file was fixed in PR #4167.
//
// No inputs and no RNG: the test points are derived from the e4m3 format
// itself. For every pair of adjacent representable e4m3 values the probe
// tests the rounding midpoint and up to 8 f32-ulps to either side (a double
// rounding through bf16 can only disagree with the direct conversion at
// these boundaries), plus bucket-interior control points where both casts
// must agree.

#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstdio>
#include <vector>

#include <cuda_fp8.h>
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

#include "tensorrt_llm/common/cudaTypeUtils.cuh"

using tensorrt_llm::common::cuda_cast;

__global__ void probe(float2 const *__restrict__ in, int n,
                      unsigned char *__restrict__ vendored,
                      unsigned char *__restrict__ direct) {
  int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
  if (i >= n) return;
  float2 const v = in[i];
  __nv_fp8x2_e4m3 const a = cuda_cast<__nv_fp8x2_e4m3>(v);
  __nv_fp8x2_e4m3 b;
  b.__x = __nv_cvt_float2_to_fp8x2(v, __NV_SATFINITE, __NV_E4M3);
  vendored[i] = (unsigned char)a.__x;
  direct[i] = (unsigned char)b.__x;
}

// OCP e4m3: 3-bit mantissa, exponent bias 7, no infinities, S.1111.111 = NaN.
static float e4m3_decode(unsigned char code) {
  int const exp = (code >> 3) & 0xF;
  int const man = code & 7;
  if (exp == 0xF && man == 0x7) return NAN;
  float const mag =
      exp == 0 ? ldexpf((float)man, -9) : ldexpf((float)(8 + man), exp - 10);
  return (code & 0x80) ? -mag : mag;
}

static float ulp_step(float v, int n) {
  int const steps = n < 0 ? -n : n;
  float const target = n < 0 ? -INFINITY : INFINITY;
  for (int i = 0; i < steps; ++i) v = nextafterf(v, target);
  return v;
}

static std::vector<float> g_vals;  // all finite e4m3 values, sorted

static size_t e4m3_index(unsigned char byte) {
  float const v = e4m3_decode(byte);
  return (size_t)(std::lower_bound(g_vals.begin(), g_vals.end(), v) -
                  g_vals.begin());
}

static void report(char const *name, std::vector<float> const &points) {
  size_t const n = points.size();
  std::vector<float2> h(n);
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) h[i] = make_float2(points[i], points[i]);

  float2 *d_in;
  unsigned char *d_v, *d_d;
  cudaMalloc(&d_in, n * sizeof(float2));
  cudaMalloc(&d_v, n);
  cudaMalloc(&d_d, n);
  cudaMemcpy(d_in, h.data(), n * sizeof(float2), cudaMemcpyHostToDevice);
  probe<<<(int)((n + 255) / 256), 256>>>(d_in, (int)n, d_v, d_d);
  cudaError_t const err = cudaDeviceSynchronize();
  if (err != cudaSuccess) {
    printf("CUDA error: %s\n", cudaGetErrorString(err));
    exit(1);
  }
  std::vector<unsigned char> vendored(n), direct(n);
  cudaMemcpy(vendored.data(), d_v, n, cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost);
  cudaMemcpy(direct.data(), d_d, n, cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost);
  cudaFree(d_in);
  cudaFree(d_v);
  cudaFree(d_d);

  size_t mism = 0, shown = 0, max_steps = 0;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    if (vendored[i] == direct[i]) continue;
    size_t const a = e4m3_index(vendored[i]), b = e4m3_index(direct[i]);
    max_steps = std::max(max_steps, a > b ? a - b : b - a);
    if (shown < 6) {
      ++shown;
      printf("  v=%+.9g  vendored=0x%02x (%+.9g)  direct=0x%02x (%+.9g)\n",
             points[i], vendored[i], e4m3_decode(vendored[i]), direct[i],
             e4m3_decode(direct[i]));
    }
    ++mism;
  }
  printf("%s points: %zu, vendored != direct: %zu (%.2f%%), "
         "max step distance: %zu\n\n",
         name, n, mism, 100.0 * (double)mism / (double)n, max_steps);
}

int main() {
  for (int c = 0; c < 256; ++c) {
    float const v = e4m3_decode((unsigned char)c);
    if (std::isfinite(v)) g_vals.push_back(v);
  }
  std::sort(g_vals.begin(), g_vals.end());
  g_vals.erase(std::unique(g_vals.begin(), g_vals.end()), g_vals.end());
  printf("finite e4m3 values: %zu, adjacent pairs: %zu\n\n", g_vals.size(),
         g_vals.size() - 1);

  std::vector<float> boundary, control;
  for (size_t k = 0; k + 1 < g_vals.size(); ++k) {
    float const lo = g_vals[k], hi = g_vals[k + 1];
    float const mid = 0.5f * (lo + hi);
    for (int o = -8; o <= 8; ++o) boundary.push_back(ulp_step(mid, o));
    control.push_back(0.5f * (lo + mid));  // bucket interior, both sides
    control.push_back(0.5f * (mid + hi));
  }

  report("boundary", boundary);
  report("control", control);
  return 0;
}
```

</details>

## Additional context

- The suggested fix is to apply the same change as #4167 to the vendored copy, which keeps the two files in sync. The change is three lines plus the comment.
- NVIDIA/TensorRT-LLM main still carries the same line at `cpp/tensorrt_llm/common/cudaTypeUtils.cuh:692` (checked 2026-09-11), which is where all surviving copies of this conversion originate. The same sync is worth doing there.
- If removing `csrc/nv_internal` entirely (#1655) is still the plan, this report is one more item for that list until it lands.
